@@ -0,0 +1,203 @@
+# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next mono decode on gfx950: what improved it, stage by stage
+
+For the monokernel author. Baseline: ATOM `qwen38-megakernels` (352229d6) FlyDSL per-layer mono path, which reproduces the public blog.
+
+Ratios only (< 1 is faster; "vs mono" = against the unmodified mono path). Everything listed as applied is **bitwise-equal to mono at layer level**, and with its knob off the ISA is byte-identical to mono. Every win beat a mono-vs-mono control (shuffled interleaved arms).
+
+Ledgers with full data: `reports/qwen38-rocke/ledger/{schedule,fused-moe,gdn-b3,head,hybrid,moe,layerkernel}.md` in the rocKE work's report tree (not in this repository). To reproduce the applied changes: [Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-rocKE.md](Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-rocKE.md).
+
+## Headline: composed and served
+
+MTP K=3, gsm8k, single request, 6 shuffled pairs:
+
+| Step (cumulative) | Verify-round time vs mono | Per-request tok/s vs mono | Increment |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1. NUMA mailboxes + stage holds (`ATOM_Q38_SCHED=best`) | 0.930 | 1.079 | 0.930 |
+| 2. + fused MoE (m3+m4+m5 as one rocKE stage) | 0.877 | 1.139 | 0.943 |
+| 3. + B3 (gated delta rule, rocKE) | 0.866 | 1.159 | 0.988 |
+| 4. + lm_head (rocKE MXFP4 GEMV + fused argmax) | **0.856** | **1.170** | 0.987 |
+
+- Acceptance is unchanged in every arm (within 0.5 %).
+- A quick 32-prompt check gave the same ~1.17x tok/s.
+
+## Where mono's time went (measured before any change)
+
+**Round composition (k=3):**
+- 0.79 target layer kernels
+- 0.02 in-graph launch boundaries
+- 0.11–0.14 non-layer work (draft steps, heads, meta)
+- ~0.01 GPU idle
+- not host-bound
+
+**The target pass:**
+- 2.31x its issued-bytes floor, and 3.37x its unique-bytes floor.
+- Hand-off-bound stages (a1, m1, b3, c1, m3, a2) spent 0.5–0.9 of their span polling, and serial stages held 0.42 of the round. The CTA x time heat map shows 0.60–0.75 of CTA time spent polling.
+- **Causal (Coz-style) slopes** of d(layer)/d(stage-entry delay):
+  - a1 ≈ 1;
+  - b1 ≈ −1.04 (r1), i.e. delaying B1's start *helps*;
+  - m3 ≈ −0.5; m4 ≈ −0.5 (r1); m1 ≈ −0.2…−0.4; q1 ≈ −0.44.
+  - Negative slopes mean early starters steal bandwidth from critical-path work.
+- **Blame graph:** mailbox hops are 0.26–0.55 of the critical path, independent of placement.
+- **Ping-pong:** a mailbox homed on the remote IOD costs 1.49–1.50x the round trip of a local one.
+
+## 1. Schedule levers (apply directly to mono's own kernel; ATOM knob `ATOM_Q38_SCHED`)
+
+| Lever | Mechanism | Result (layer vs mono unless noted) | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **`numa=1`: dual-copy NUMA mailboxes** | Every mailbox is written to two copies, one homed on each IOD (per-2 MiB-page home-IOD table calibrated at init: page mask from bit-13 rule, agreement 1.0000). Consumers poll the IOD-local copy. | delta r1 0.889–0.890; delta r4s 0.917–0.923; qsa r1 0.917; qsa r4s 0.949. **Served k=3 round 0.934.** Ablations: layout alone (always copy 0) 1.004–1.010, always the remote copy 0.987–1.008, so the whole gain is local polling. | **applied** |
+| `hold.b1=32` | B1 tasks sleep 32 units at entry so they don't contend with a1's critical loads | r1 0.982 (16: 0.987, 64: 0.987, 128: 1.036) | applied r1 |
+| `hold.m3=N` | delay router entry | delta r4s 128: 0.989 (256: 1.011); qsa r4s 64: 0.995 | applied r4s |
+| `hold.m4=64` | delay M4 entry | r1 0.991; with hold.b1: 0.973 | applied r1 |
+| `hold.m1=32` | delay M1 entry | qsa r4s 0.998 | applied qsa r4s |
+| `head_sleep=128` (mono's HEAD_SLEEP was 64) | a1 head CTAs back off longer | r1 0.993, r4s 0.997, qsa 0.995; 0 is a LOSS (1.002–1.018) | applied |
+
+**Best sets on top of numa** (`sched.BEST`):
+
+| Layer | Holds | vs mono |
+|---|---|---|
+| delta r1 | b1=32, m4=64, m3=32 | **0.858–0.860** |
+| delta r4s | m3=128, head_sleep=128 | **0.907–0.913** |
+| qsa r4s | m3=64, head_sleep=128, m1=32 | **0.939–0.942** |
+
+- Served k=3 round **0.928**.
+- Bitwise on all 21 narrow variants and on the grouped builds; neutral at concurrency 4.
+
+**Tried and lost (kept as off knobs; don't spend time on these):**
+
+| Lever | vs mono / effect | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `pause=N` (s_sleep between polls) | 1.002–1.010 | |
+| `pipe=N` (two poll loads in flight) | 1.003–1.032 | |
+| `prio` (s_setprio) | no effect | one CTA per CU, nothing to arbitrate |
+| `place.m1` placement | 1.041 | the simulator predicted 0.956; simulator placement predictions are not trustworthy |
+| B3 state prefetch by B1's idle waves | +0.2–1.0 % slower | |
+| **2-layer stacked launch** | 1.024–1.040 | real-layer fences and dependencies cost more than the inter-layer grid sync; this is despite 0.54 on a toy chain |
+| **dynamic ticket task assignment** | 1.012–1.079 | |
+
+## 2. MoE: m3 + m4 + m5 fused into one expert-major stage (rocKE `moe_fused.py`, linked in place of mono's m3/m4/m5)
+
+**Why mono's MoE is slow (rows 4):**
+- Two weight streams with a hop between them; M5 issue is gated on M4.
+- An all-to-all `dn` poll of ~59 KB per CTA, about 9.4 MB on the critical path.
+- Routing and slot tables are rebuilt per stage, each with a barrier.
+- Each verify row re-loads the same experts: 107.6 MB issued vs ~80 MB unique, with U ≈ 28.5 unique experts per 40.
+- At rows 1 it under-fills: 110 / 160 CTAs, below the ~192-CTA saturation point.
+
+**Fused design:**
+- **Slots:** slot 0 = shared expert (static), slots 1..U = union of the rows' top-10, sorted by id.
+- **Work units:**
+  - gu units (slot x 32-column block of the intermediate, so MXFP8 amax/E8M0 stays inside one CTA);
+  - dn units (slot x 16 hidden columns).
+- **Placement (`map=line`):** gu and dn units laid out on a balanced closed-form byte line per IOD. Every CTA streams gate_up then down of the same expert(s), with equal bytes per CTA, no tables, and one barrier after routing.
+- **Exchange:** the intermediate goes through mono's own `dn` mailbox (same bits), read within the same IOD.
+- **Combine:** 160 tile tasks run mono's rank-ordered combine verbatim.
+- **Bitwise:** no K-split, no atomics.
+
+**Fused MoE levers** (chosen by hill climbing with a lookback over retained knobs):
+
+| Lever | Result | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| W0 null-byte build (measure fixed overhead first) | fixed overhead 0.625 (r4s) / 0.845 (r1) of mono MoE, ~3x the model; the router + routing window (~0.26–0.28) gates every routed byte | finding |
+| down weights issued **after** the gate_up epilogue | removes VGPR spills (first light with design defaults was 1.18 because of spills) | applied |
+| invalid chains / down units issue no loads | ATT: issue phase was 75–85 % load-issue stalls | applied |
+| `xchg_pre=none` + router with mono's own load/mailbox settings | r4s 0.942 vs 0.945 | applied rows > 1 |
+| rows 1: arithmetic slots, `groups` map, 32-CTA shared group at base 160 | r1 layer 0.972 (MoE 0.914) | applied rows 1 |
+| `numa=1` + `lg_copy=numa2` (+ `xchg_home`/`ys_home=numa2` at r1), `line_order=router_dn` | built on top of the schedule lever | applied |
+| issue down weights during the gate_up stream | 0.970–0.977; gate_up must go first because the exchange waits on it | lost |
+| shared group combines everything (`combine=sh`) | 0.986 | lost |
+| `spec=prev` (prefetch last step's experts into MALL) | MoE 1.20–1.26 even with a perfect oracle: the per-CU stream is already bandwidth-bound | lost |
+| polling throttles, `extras=late`, `iod_split=0`, other shared-group sizes, `w_cpol` 0/16 | 0.955–0.98 | lost |
+
+**MoE results:**
+- r4s MoE 0.874–0.887 of mono; 0.78 with correlated rows (MTP), 0.835–0.844 uncorrelated.
+- r1 0.885–0.922.
+- After routing the stream runs within ~10 % of full-grid HBM bandwidth (FETCH ≈ unique bytes, 0.78 of mono's).
+- **What's left is the router window.**
+- Served on its own over plain mono: k=3 0.956, k=0 0.979.
+
+**Bugs caught:**
+- A private-mailbox tag of `tag | 2^31` collides with a launch tag at step 2^25. Use `tag ^ 2^31`; a test crosses the boundary.
+- An rows-3 LDS buffer overflow (fixed; CPU guard added).
+
+## 3. B3: gated delta rule (rocKE `stage_b3.py`, linked in place of mono's B3; b3pre runs inside B1)
+
+**Mono B3 (stamps):**
+- 0.13–0.14 of a delta-layer launch.
+- At r2s, 39 % of B3's time is in `s_barrier` and 15 % in `vmcnt` waits.
+
+| Lever (`B3Knobs`) | Mechanism | Result vs current best | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **`state_nt=True`** | non-temporal stores of the f32 recurrent-state snapshot (avoid polluting L2/MALL for the following M4/M5 streams) | r4s 0.9695 (largest single B3 lever) | applied |
+| **`pre=dma`** (b3pre in B1) | state block + b/a weights staged into LDS by DMA during B1, overlapped with B1's GEMM | r1 0.9975 | applied r1 (r3/r4 don't fit LDS) |
+| **`mb_impl=ir`** | compiler-visible tagged poll instead of an asm loop | r1 0.9967, r4s 0.9895 | applied |
+| **`split=2`** | 2 slices per value head instead of 4: fewer exchange hops, more work per CTA | r1 0.9875 | applied r1 |
+| **`head_map=xcd`** | place the sibling CTAs of a head across XCDs | r4s 0.995 | applied r4s |
+| `wait=barrier`, `state_issue=entry`, `hoist=gates_z`, `xchg=numa2` | wait form, early state-load issue, gate hoisting, dual-copy exchange | 0.996–0.9975 each | applied per variant |
+| `stores=after_put/end`, `core_put=direct`, `norms=redundant`, `barrier=full`, `table=global`, `shfl4=swizzle`, `cvt=intrinsic`, `mb_pause` | — | 0.999–1.04 | lost / neutral |
+
+**Tuned sets:**
+
+| Variant | Set | Delta layer vs mono |
+|---|---|---|
+| r1 | K1 = split=2, wait=barrier, state_nt, state_issue=entry, xchg=numa2, hoist=gates_z, pre=dma, mb_impl=ir | 0.953–0.957 |
+| r2s | K2S = split=1, state_nt, pre=dma, mb_impl=ir | 0.940–0.942 |
+| r4s | K4S = head_map=xcd, wait=barrier, state_nt, state_issue=entry, mb_impl=ir, mb_pause=none | 0.965–0.967 |
+
+- Grouped r4g2 is bitwise and served.
+- On top of the NUMA lever (B3 made NUMA-aware): delta layer 0.858 (r1) / 0.883–0.886 (r4s) of plain mono, i.e. 0.963–0.970 of mono+numa.
+- Served: k=0 per token 0.974, k=1 0.971, k=3 round 0.987 (alone).
+
+## 4. lm_head (rocKE `mx_decode_gemv.py`; ATOM `ATOM_Q38_ROCKE_HEAD=1` replaces `_mxfp4_head` + `_argmax_fold`)
+
+**Why mono's head is slow** (PMC/ATT):
+- It fetches 1.23x the head's bytes.
+- It spends 45–47 % of its time WAITing at `vmcnt(0)` + `s_barrier`.
+- rocKE fetches exactly 1.00x and streams 0.87 of its time, vs mono 0.72–0.75.
+
+| Lever | Result | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| persistent CTAs; panel = 1 x 16 columns x K (`nt=1`); x in LDS; mono's MFMA order (bitwise logits) | r1 0.827–0.840, r4 0.862 (logits) | applied |
+| prologue order (scales first, sched_barrier per prologue group) | loop-top wait vmcnt(10) → vmcnt(19–21) | applied |
+| **`ctas=243`** (tail balance: 15520 panels / 972 wave slots) | argmax r1 0.783, r4 0.772 (from 0.814 / 0.795 at 256) | applied |
+| one-launch fused argmax fold (epoch counter, system-scope atomics) | — | applied |
+| `waves_per_eu=1` on argmax builds | −0.4 % | applied |
+| ctypes launcher arguments built once | host cost of eager draft-head launches | applied |
+| serpentine column order across draft heads (MALL reuse) | e2e 0.991 vs 0.985 | lost |
+
+**lm_head results:**
+- Kernel 0.77–0.84 of mono.
+- Served k=3 round 0.987 (6 pairs, sd 0.003); k=0 neutral.
+
+**Bug caught:** the argmax LDS reduction buffer was overlaid on the x buffer by liveness packing. Waves leave the loop at different times, so this corrupted x; it is now `exclusive=True` with a regression test.
+
+## 5. Other measured facts useful to the author
+
+**Hop latency and building blocks:**
+- Hop latency (ping-pong / all-to-all) with rocKE's asm and IR mailboxes is at parity with mono's `put`/`poll_raw` (0.94–1.10).
+- Kernel-argument preload removes a 44 % kernarg-wait stall at stage entry on the toy chain. Mono doesn't preload; this is untested on the real layer.
+- DPP wave sums instead of LDS swizzle remove a 3–7 % gap on the toy chain.
+- The scaled MFMA 16x16x128 and 32x32x64 (fp8 x fp4) have equal peak FLOP throughput. 16x16x128 issues twice as often with half the work each, which suits decode GEMVs with ≤ 16 rows.
+
+**Correctness and determinism:**
+- Hazard: an inline-asm MFMA immediately after a VALU write of its operands returns wrong results on gfx950 without `s_nop` padding.
+- Unmodified mono served text is not deterministic run to run (0/8 and 4–17 of 144–480 prompts identical). Prove numerics at layer level, not with served text.
+
+**Timing:**
+- Timing stamps bias results (QSA r4s read 0.988 stamped vs 0.996–1.008 unstamped). Fixed-order or best-of-N arm selection produced false wins (a claimed 0.993 for Q2 was withdrawn).
+- Use shuffled arms, a mono-vs-mono control, a recompiled-mono control, and require a win to beat the control's minimum in ≥ 3/4 of rounds.
+
+## 6. What's left (largest first)
+
+**Serial stages still on mono code** (~0.4 of the round): a1, m1, c1, b1, a2, m2, the q-chain, PLE.
+- Levers to try: post-wait tails across all 8 waves, kernarg preload, MX GEMV tiles like the lm_head kernel's, B1's verify conv window.
+
+**Router window of the fused MoE** (~0.07 of the round at rows 4).
+
+**Non-layer time outside the graph** (0.11–0.14 of the round).
+- Graph-capture the draft step (eager today because of mRoPE).
+- Fold front/meta into the layer kernel.
+- Device-side accepted-count handling (removes the per-step host round trip).
+
+**Not yet covered by rocKE:** grouped / wide builds (8–16 rows, concurrency > 1) still run mono's MoE and B3.
+
+**Gains overlap at MTP 3:** composition reached ~80 % of the summed gains before NUMA. B3's `state_nt` and MoE's cache-policy streams relieve the same L2/MALL pressure.
